{
  "body_human_readable": "When working with ontologies, several specialized tools known as Ontology Development Environments (ODEs) are commonly used. These tools facilitate the creation, editing, maintenance, and integration of ontologies. The most popular and widely used ODEs include:\n\n1. **Prot\u00e9g\u00e9**: An open-source ontology editor and knowledge store developed at Stanford. It is highly suitable for editing ontologies, offering modules for active ontology creation, entity management, property assertion, and integration with various plugins (e.g., OWL Viz, DL Query). Prot\u00e9g\u00e9 is considered the most complete and popular option, especially in biomedical informatics and knowledge bases. It also supports RDF/XML and OWL/XML formats and provides extensive documentation and plugin support for scalability and integration [1] [2] [3].\n\n2. **TopBraid Composer**: A visual, all-encompassing tool for building and maintaining domain models and ontologies in the Semantic Web context. It supports RDF, RDFS, and OWL, and offers an open plugin structure for extensibility. It is used for both client/server and file/database-based ontology management [4] [3].\n\n3. **OntoStudio**: A multilingual editor and ontology modeler with customization options. It supports OWL, RDF, and ObjectLogic formats, and allows users to store data in various file formats and repositories. It includes a query builder for SQL databases and supports collaborative server-based ontology management [4] [5].\n\n4. **Fluent Editor**: An open-source tool that allows editing ontologies in natural language and supports interoperability with Prot\u00e9g\u00e9. It provides interactive diagrams, SWRL debugging, and integration with R language for statistical analysis of ontologies [6].\n\n5. **VocBench**: An open-source ODE used by many businesses, managing OWL ontologies, SKOS, and other RDF datasets. It is designed for collaborative editing and workflow management [7].\n\n6. **Swoop**: A web-based ontology editor for OWL, offering browser features like bookmarks, history, and hypertext navigation. It supports version control, debugging, and integration of ontology contents within the same ontology [8].\n\n7. **OBO-Edit**: A particular ontology editor for biologists, focusing on graphical editing and integration of contents within the same ontology. It is less generic and more specialized for biological ontologies [9].\n\nOther notable tools include KAON2 and WebODE, which have been important in the past for ontology development [7].\n\nThese tools are evaluated based on usability, scalability, stability, integration, documentation, and originality. Most of them support plugins and extensions to enhance their functionality and allow integration with other systems and databases [2] [3].\n\n**Summary Table of Tools:**\n- Prot\u00e9g\u00e9\n- TopBraid Composer\n- OntoStudio\n- Fluent Editor\n- VocBench\n- Swoop\n- OBO-Edit\n- KAON2 (historical)\n- WebODE (historical)\n\nEach tool offers unique features and strengths, so the choice depends on the specific requirements of your ontology project [1] [2] [4] [9] [7].",
  "request": {
