Access document 'ontologies.pdf'\",\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":0,\"name\":\" [0.01s]\",\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":1,\"name\":\"Load indexes for 'ontologies.pdf'\",\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":1,\"content\":\"Number of chunks: 296\\n\",\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":1,\"content\":\"Total text size: 85.4 KiB\\n\",\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":1,\"name\":\" [0.16s]\",\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":1,\"status\":\"completed\"}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":2,\"name\":\"Prepare indexes for search\",\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":2,\"name\":\" [0.02s]\",\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":2,\"status\":\"completed\"}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":3,\"name\":\"Standalone question\",\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":3,\"content\":\"What are the tools to use when working with ontologies?\",\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":3,\"name\":\" [0.00s]\",\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":3,\"status\":\"completed\"}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":4,\"name\":\"Combined search\",\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":5,\"name\":\"Embeddings search\",\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"name\":\"Keywords search\",\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":7,\"name\":\"Page image search\",\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":0,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.229] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"equipped with the capability to shape and combine various ontologies, thus ensuring the potential of developing shared and reusable informa- tion archives. Swoop\\u2019s predisposition to integration is less clear; it fol- lows di\\ufb00erent principles. It has commands for the fusion of ontologies, but developers were mainly concerned with operations for the reuse of contents within the same ontology: the Swoop \\u201cResource Holder \\u201d tool. OBO-Edit integrations also regard contents in the same ontology. On- toStudio allows the development and maintenance of ontologies by us- ing graphical means, based on client/server architecture. In OntoStudio the ontologies are managed in a central server and users can access and redesign the ontologies. TopBraid Composer (FE) may use in a single user mode working with ontologies stored as \\ufb01les or in a database.\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=8\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":1,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.67] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Fluent Editor [28] is an entirely free and open source. It o\\ufb00ers Pro- t\\u00e9g\\u00e9 interoperability by installing the necessary plug-in, allowing users to edit ontologies in natural language and working with the majority of the Semantic Web W3C standard [106] . Using the selected materializa- tion pro\\ufb01le, the user can display the ontology as an interactive diagram and immediately analyze the results. Fluent Editor gives the user access to the SWRL Debugger, a tool for creating actual ontologies and express- ing intricate conditional relationships as examples. The \\ufb01rst iteration of an embedded SWRL debugger has been made available by the develop- ers to assist users while they model with SWRL. It enables the user to see which rules were put into action during materialization and which entities were employed in place of their head clauses and body. The user will concentrate on the real meaning of the ontology being updated (e.g., taxonomy, vocabulary, rule set). Using an R language program to\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=5\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":2,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.230] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"3.8.5. Documentation\\n\\nThe Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 user manual is relatively straightforward; it contains an introduction that explains all theoretical and conceptual aspects of ontologies and languages for the semantic web. This site has produced good, comprehensive updated work, becoming thus a great resource for all Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 users. Swoop, OntoStudio and TopBraid Composer (FE) ed- itors provide documentation ontology and ontology libraries. A user\\u2019s guide to the program is provided by Fluent Editor, VonBench and OBO- Edit. The huge and well-organized materials are made possible by frame- works with hypertext links.\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=8\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":3,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.78] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Swoop has Blackbox, a method to diagnose entities. Reasoners are tasked to develop a set of domains, in this method; the ontology struc- ture is used to pinpoint a problem\\u2019s root cause. In case of con\\ufb02ict, the program displays the event reason in the o\\ufb00ending entity description \\ufb01eld with the use of a semi-formal language and the insertion of some accountable axioms. Here, we can use the Run Debug Tests, Debugging / Explanation, and Repair Ontology commands in the Advanced tab. We can use Version Control to maintain our ontologies appropriately and to verify that their entities are used correctly. This tool o\\ufb00ers two sorts of operations, changelogs and checkpoints; it allows users to keep vari- ous iterations of the same ontology. Changelogs describe the ontology\\u2019s evolutionary route and list changes and adjustments in chronological order. Checkpoints provide quick switching between versions, showcas- ing the range of current models. Swoop supports all RDFS/OWL rela- tional constructs\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=6\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":4,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.98] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Scalability is when applications can integrate software packages or plug-ins. All the programs examined in this study are planned intending to allow additional supports and/or plug-ins to enhance their perfor- mance. Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 is certainly the program with greater potential improve- ment because of its extensive use; it is the most signi\\ufb01cant product in the industry; many institutions are working on initiatives to develop plug-ins for this software package. Therefore, it has taken the lead in the research and education \\ufb01elds. The Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 plugin library contains several items; one of such plugins is SKOSed [144] . Swoop, Fluent Editor and VocBench are also working to develop plug-ins, dealing with large amount of data, while still o\\ufb00ering a friendly environment. OBO-Edit is the only program that handles this problem less, as it was designed for a speci\\ufb01c ontology rather than for being a generic editor. TopBraid Com- poser is based on an open plugin structure. Advanced features in this\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=7\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":5,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.8] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Ontologies are increasingly being employed to enhance understand- ing and transferability of knowledge in Life Science [1\\u20137] . Research groups are interested in developing ontologies [8] to arrange and con- nect data into searchable libraries [ 9 , 10 ], to establish frameworks of essential concepts (e.g., OWS framework [11] ), to share information, to use knowledge to minimize confusion [12] and to allocate the grow- ing volume and data multiplicity in biomedical research, chemistry, medicine, genomics, biology and pharmacology [13] . Further reasons for appreciating its utility are a wider understanding of the value of standardized vocabularies and codi\\ufb01ed semantics in various domains [8] and the development or extension of construction tools. To enable widespread access [14] and give users the ability to publish, explore, create and update, a few works have been published regarding pro- cedures [15\\u201318] plans, criteria, activities and methodologies adapted to build them. The state of\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=1\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":6,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.248] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Declaration of Competing Interest\\n\\nThe authors declare that they have no known competing \\ufb01nancial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to in\\ufb02uence the work reported in this paper.\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=11\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"name\":\" [0.00s]\",\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"status\":\"completed\"}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":5,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":0,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.63] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"OntoStudio 3.2.0 [27] has been the widely used and accepted tool for building and maintaining ontologies a few years ago [106] . Its prede- cessor version was called OntoEdit [131] . It is a multilingual editor and ontology modeler with a lot of customization options. French, English and German are the languages that can de\\ufb01ne synonyms. Classes, prop- erties, rules, queries and mapping are all part of it. This editor supports the OWL, RDF and ObjectLogic formats [27] . When starting a project, users have the option to choose their preferred storage type, includ- ing internal repositories or a collaborative server. Di\\ufb00erent \\ufb01le formats, such as xml, html, doc, ppt, pdf and postscript, can store data. With the use of a query builder, it provides the option to get data from databases, such as a SQL database. The availability of Query Builder SQL makes it simple to design queries. An external OntoStudio support tool called On- toBroker uses ObjectLogic and SPARQL to query the graph. SPARQL\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=5\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":5,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":1,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.282] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"reference manual. 1992.\\n\\n[108] Farquhar A, Fikes R, Rice J. The ontolingua server: A tool for collaborative ontology\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=12\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":5,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":2,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.48] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Ontology learning is becoming a sub-area of ontology engineering due to the exponential growth of web documents and the cutting-edge techniques shared by the machine learning, natural language process- ing, information retrieval, and arti\\ufb01cial intelligence communities. Ex- isting ontologies have generally been created manually. This method of creating ontology has traditionally been used by most ontology en- gineers, even if this procedure is exceedingly error-prone and time- consuming which makes it di\\ufb03cult to maintain and update ontologies. Due of this, researchers have constantly explored new approaches to produce or maintain and update ontologies in both e\\ufb03cient and ef- fective ways [127] . This survey aims to highlight the main contribu- tions on ontology generation, providing descriptions of the most pop- ular and widely used ontology development environments (ODEs), in- cluding Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 [128] , Topbraid Composer [26] , Ontostudio [27] , Flu- ent Editor [28] , VocBench [29] ,\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=4\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":5,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":3,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.290] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"scription logics. Handbook Automated Reason 2001;2:1581\\u2013634 .\\n\\n[126] Baader F, Horrocks I, Sattler U. Description logics. In: Handbook on ontologies.\\n\\nSpringer; 2004. p. 3\\u201328 .\\n\\n[127] Hee Hwang C. Incompletely and imprecisely speaking: using dynamic ontologies for representing and retrieving information. In: KRDB, 21. Citeseer; 1999. p. 14\\u201320 . [128] Musen MA. The Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 project: a look back and a look forward. AI Matters\\n\\n2015;1(4):4\\u201312 .\\n\\n[129] Tudorache T, Vendetti J, Noy NFridman. Webprotege: A lightweight owl ontology\\n\\neditor for the web. In: OWLED, 432; 2008. p. 2009 .\\n\\n[130] McGuinness DL, Fikes R, Rice J, Wilder S. The chimaera ontology environment.\\n\\nAAAI/IAAI 2000;2000:1123\\u20134 .\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=13\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":5,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":4,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.230] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"3.8.5. Documentation\\n\\nThe Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 user manual is relatively straightforward; it contains an introduction that explains all theoretical and conceptual aspects of ontologies and languages for the semantic web. This site has produced good, comprehensive updated work, becoming thus a great resource for all Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 users. Swoop, OntoStudio and TopBraid Composer (FE) ed- itors provide documentation ontology and ontology libraries. A user\\u2019s guide to the program is provided by Fluent Editor, VonBench and OBO- Edit. The huge and well-organized materials are made possible by frame- works with hypertext links.\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=8\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":5,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":5,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.245] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Fig. 7. OntoGraph relationships.\\n\\ngeneral characteristics and common knowledge regarding experimental studies, as well as to allow the communication among users, opening the opportunity to explore e-science concepts into the LS experimenta- tion domain. Potential future research directions would be to automate the process of creating the ontology. Beyond the creation of new on- tologies, another challenging area of work in the subject of ontologies is the evolution and mapping of current ontologies. Due to the cost of cre- ation, abstraction, and reusability, this is signi\\ufb01cant. Di\\ufb00erent types of ontologies can be derived from a core ontology to suit particular appli-\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=10\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":5,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":6,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.14] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Ontologies\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=2\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":5,\"name\":\" [0.02s]\",\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":5,\"status\":\"completed\"}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":7,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":0,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.44] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 4]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=4\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":7,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":1,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.45] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 4]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=4\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":7,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":2,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.46] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 4]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=4\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":7,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":3,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.47] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 4]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=4\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":7,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":4,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.48] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 4]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=4\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":7,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":5,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.49] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 4]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=4\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":7,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":6,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.50] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 4]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=4\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":7,\"name\":\" [0.02s]\",\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":7,\"status\":\"completed\"}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":4,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":0,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.48] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Ontology learning is becoming a sub-area of ontology engineering due to the exponential growth of web documents and the cutting-edge techniques shared by the machine learning, natural language process- ing, information retrieval, and arti\\ufb01cial intelligence communities. Ex- isting ontologies have generally been created manually. This method of creating ontology has traditionally been used by most ontology en- gineers, even if this procedure is exceedingly error-prone and time- consuming which makes it di\\ufb03cult to maintain and update ontologies. Due of this, researchers have constantly explored new approaches to produce or maintain and update ontologies in both e\\ufb03cient and ef- fective ways [127] . This survey aims to highlight the main contribu- tions on ontology generation, providing descriptions of the most pop- ular and widely used ontology development environments (ODEs), in- cluding Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 [128] , Topbraid Composer [26] , Ontostudio [27] , Flu- ent Editor [28] , VocBench [29] ,\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=4\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":4,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":1,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.230] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"3.8.5. Documentation\\n\\nThe Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 user manual is relatively straightforward; it contains an introduction that explains all theoretical and conceptual aspects of ontologies and languages for the semantic web. This site has produced good, comprehensive updated work, becoming thus a great resource for all Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 users. Swoop, OntoStudio and TopBraid Composer (FE) ed- itors provide documentation ontology and ontology libraries. A user\\u2019s guide to the program is provided by Fluent Editor, VonBench and OBO- Edit. The huge and well-organized materials are made possible by frame- works with hypertext links.\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=8\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":4,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":2,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.63] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"OntoStudio 3.2.0 [27] has been the widely used and accepted tool for building and maintaining ontologies a few years ago [106] . Its prede- cessor version was called OntoEdit [131] . It is a multilingual editor and ontology modeler with a lot of customization options. French, English and German are the languages that can de\\ufb01ne synonyms. Classes, prop- erties, rules, queries and mapping are all part of it. This editor supports the OWL, RDF and ObjectLogic formats [27] . When starting a project, users have the option to choose their preferred storage type, includ- ing internal repositories or a collaborative server. Di\\ufb00erent \\ufb01le formats, such as xml, html, doc, ppt, pdf and postscript, can store data. With the use of a query builder, it provides the option to get data from databases, such as a SQL database. The availability of Query Builder SQL makes it simple to design queries. An external OntoStudio support tool called On- toBroker uses ObjectLogic and SPARQL to query the graph. SPARQL\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=5\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":4,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":3,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.229] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"equipped with the capability to shape and combine various ontologies, thus ensuring the potential of developing shared and reusable informa- tion archives. Swoop\\u2019s predisposition to integration is less clear; it fol- lows di\\ufb00erent principles. It has commands for the fusion of ontologies, but developers were mainly concerned with operations for the reuse of contents within the same ontology: the Swoop \\u201cResource Holder \\u201d tool. OBO-Edit integrations also regard contents in the same ontology. On- toStudio allows the development and maintenance of ontologies by us- ing graphical means, based on client/server architecture. In OntoStudio the ontologies are managed in a central server and users can access and redesign the ontologies. TopBraid Composer (FE) may use in a single user mode working with ontologies stored as \\ufb01les or in a database.\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=8\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":4,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":4,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.44] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 4]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=4\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":4,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":5,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.282] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"reference manual. 1992.\\n\\n[108] Farquhar A, Fikes R, Rice J. The ontolingua server: A tool for collaborative ontology\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=12\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":4,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":6,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.67] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Fluent Editor [28] is an entirely free and open source. It o\\ufb00ers Pro- t\\u00e9g\\u00e9 interoperability by installing the necessary plug-in, allowing users to edit ontologies in natural language and working with the majority of the Semantic Web W3C standard [106] . Using the selected materializa- tion pro\\ufb01le, the user can display the ontology as an interactive diagram and immediately analyze the results. Fluent Editor gives the user access to the SWRL Debugger, a tool for creating actual ontologies and express- ing intricate conditional relationships as examples. The \\ufb01rst iteration of an embedded SWRL debugger has been made available by the develop- ers to assist users while they model with SWRL. It enables the user to see which rules were put into action during materialization and which entities were employed in place of their head clauses and body. The user will concentrate on the real meaning of the ontology being updated (e.g., taxonomy, vocabulary, rule set). Using an R language program to\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=5\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":4,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":7,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.45] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 4]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=4\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":4,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":8,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.46] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 4]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=4\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":4,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":9,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.290] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"scription logics. Handbook Automated Reason 2001;2:1581\\u2013634 .\\n\\n[126] Baader F, Horrocks I, Sattler U. Description logics. In: Handbook on ontologies.\\n\\nSpringer; 2004. p. 3\\u201328 .\\n\\n[127] Hee Hwang C. Incompletely and imprecisely speaking: using dynamic ontologies for representing and retrieving information. In: KRDB, 21. Citeseer; 1999. p. 14\\u201320 . [128] Musen MA. The Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 project: a look back and a look forward. AI Matters\\n\\n2015;1(4):4\\u201312 .\\n\\n[129] Tudorache T, Vendetti J, Noy NFridman. Webprotege: A lightweight owl ontology\\n\\neditor for the web. In: OWLED, 432; 2008. p. 2009 .\\n\\n[130] McGuinness DL, Fikes R, Rice J, Wilder S. The chimaera ontology environment.\\n\\nAAAI/IAAI 2000;2000:1123\\u20134 .\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=13\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":4,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":10,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.78] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Swoop has Blackbox, a method to diagnose entities. Reasoners are tasked to develop a set of domains, in this method; the ontology struc- ture is used to pinpoint a problem\\u2019s root cause. In case of con\\ufb02ict, the program displays the event reason in the o\\ufb00ending entity description \\ufb01eld with the use of a semi-formal language and the insertion of some accountable axioms. Here, we can use the Run Debug Tests, Debugging / Explanation, and Repair Ontology commands in the Advanced tab. We can use Version Control to maintain our ontologies appropriately and to verify that their entities are used correctly. This tool o\\ufb00ers two sorts of operations, changelogs and checkpoints; it allows users to keep vari- ous iterations of the same ontology. Changelogs describe the ontology\\u2019s evolutionary route and list changes and adjustments in chronological order. Checkpoints provide quick switching between versions, showcas- ing the range of current models. Swoop supports all RDFS/OWL rela- tional constructs\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=6\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":4,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":11,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.47] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 4]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=4\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":4,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":12,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.98] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Scalability is when applications can integrate software packages or plug-ins. All the programs examined in this study are planned intending to allow additional supports and/or plug-ins to enhance their perfor- mance. Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 is certainly the program with greater potential improve- ment because of its extensive use; it is the most signi\\ufb01cant product in the industry; many institutions are working on initiatives to develop plug-ins for this software package. Therefore, it has taken the lead in the research and education \\ufb01elds. The Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 plugin library contains several items; one of such plugins is SKOSed [144] . Swoop, Fluent Editor and VocBench are also working to develop plug-ins, dealing with large amount of data, while still o\\ufb00ering a friendly environment. OBO-Edit is the only program that handles this problem less, as it was designed for a speci\\ufb01c ontology rather than for being a generic editor. TopBraid Com- poser is based on an open plugin structure. Advanced features in this\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=7\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":4,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":13,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.245] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Fig. 7. OntoGraph relationships.\\n\\ngeneral characteristics and common knowledge regarding experimental studies, as well as to allow the communication among users, opening the opportunity to explore e-science concepts into the LS experimenta- tion domain. Potential future research directions would be to automate the process of creating the ontology. Beyond the creation of new on- tologies, another challenging area of work in the subject of ontologies is the evolution and mapping of current ontologies. Due to the cost of cre- ation, abstraction, and reusability, this is signi\\ufb01cant. Di\\ufb00erent types of ontologies can be derived from a core ontology to suit particular appli-\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=10\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":4,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":14,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.8] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Ontologies are increasingly being employed to enhance understand- ing and transferability of knowledge in Life Science [1\\u20137] . Research groups are interested in developing ontologies [8] to arrange and con- nect data into searchable libraries [ 9 , 10 ], to establish frameworks of essential concepts (e.g., OWS framework [11] ), to share information, to use knowledge to minimize confusion [12] and to allocate the grow- ing volume and data multiplicity in biomedical research, chemistry, medicine, genomics, biology and pharmacology [13] . Further reasons for appreciating its utility are a wider understanding of the value of standardized vocabularies and codi\\ufb01ed semantics in various domains [8] and the development or extension of construction tools. To enable widespread access [14] and give users the ability to publish, explore, create and update, a few works have been published regarding pro- cedures [15\\u201318] plans, criteria, activities and methodologies adapted to build them. The state of\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=1\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":4,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":15,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.49] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 4]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=4\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":4,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":16,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.14] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Ontologies\",\"reference_url\":\"files/PHhReJdzQbtjwVegSpXV64/ontologies.pdf#page=2\"}],\"status\":null}]}}}],\"usage\":null,\"id\":\"9d8358a3-e80e-452a-a414-7333f7eecefc\",\"created\":1771936022,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\"}\n\ndata: {\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"finish_reason\":null,\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":4,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":17,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.248] ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Declaration of Competing Interest\\n\\nThe authors declare that they have no known competing \\ufb01nancial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to in\\ufb02uence the work reported